{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim depends on **which country and time period** you mean. It is not universally true.\n\nIn the United States, historical data have generally shown lower average life expectancy for Black people than for the overall population. In England and Wales, some ethnicity-based estimates have shown higher life expectancy for Black African and Black Caribbean groups than for the overall population.\n\nA precise assessment also needs to distinguish life expectancy at birth from life expectancy at a later age, and consider differences by sex and ethnic subgroup. Any observed gap does not establish an inherent biological cause; living conditions, healthcare access, discrimination, and other factors can influence it."}}
